WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.515
Do you like apples?

2
00:00:01.516 --> 00:00:06.591
If so, you have a very special man to thank─ or so legend would have you believe.

3
00:00:06.592 --> 00:00:20.007
In 1774 when America was about to become a new nation, a man named John Chapman was born─a man who would change the face of the American frontier.

4
00:00:20.008 --> 00:00:23.289
Not much is known about his childhood or his family.

5
00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:28.804
However, history books do tell us that he went to school and became very skilled with plants.

6
00:00:28.805 --> 00:00:35.173
There was one plant, however, that John preferred above all other plants: the apple tree.

7
00:00:35.174 --> 00:00:44.160
To him, the apple tree was a sign of hope─the way it blossomed in the spring and produced enough apples in the fall to feed a person for a full year.

8
00:00:44.161 --> 00:00:49.558
He was a Christian man and was interested in sharing his apple trees and his love for God.

9
00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:57.485
Since he had no wife or children, he decided to leave his home in Massachusetts and travel west to plant apple trees.

10
00:00:57.486 --> 00:01:05.024
Like many settlers, John had heard rumors that the soil out West was dark and rich and ready for a good planting.

11
00:01:05.025 --> 00:01:08.139
This was all he needed to know to pack his bag.

12
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:12.952
In his small bag, he carried a blanket, some apple seeds, his Bible, and a pot.

13
00:01:12.953 --> 00:01:17.000
It wasn't much, but it was all he needed to survive.

14
00:01:17.001 --> 00:01:23.238
John moved from town to town, from plain to plain, from wilderness to wilderness, planting apple seeds.

15
00:01:23.239 --> 00:01:27.872
First, he would look for ground that was near water so the seeds would have something to drink.

16
00:01:27.873 --> 00:01:34.068
Then he would pull out the weeds or tall grasses from the soil, move large rocks and small stones,

17
00:01:34.069 --> 00:01:39.372
and then plow the field so the seedlings could plant their roots.

18
00:01:39.373 --> 00:01:46.146
After the seedlings had broken through the soil, John would build a fence around them to keep animals from eating the baby trees.

19
00:01:46.147 --> 00:01:49.079
In time the trees would become tall and strong.

20
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.556
Then John would sell the trees to settlers as they moved west.

21
00:01:52.557 --> 00:01:57.761
The settlers would plant and eat from the trees at their new homes.

22
00:01:57.762 --> 00:02:03.652
After all of the trees were sold, John would move farther west himself and begin the plantings again.

23
00:02:03.653 --> 00:02:06.851
Many settlers paid him a penny or two for each tree.

24
00:02:06.852 --> 00:02:11.275
However, some settlers didn't have any money and could only promise to pay him later.

25
00:02:11.276 --> 00:02:18.322
Other settlers were moving on and couldn't make that promise, so they paid John with used clothing or free meals.

26
00:02:18.323 --> 00:02:21.557
John Chapman was loved by all the settlers who knew him.

27
00:02:21.558 --> 00:02:29.250
Wherever he traveled, people spoke about the Apple Tree Man or, as he is popularly known, Johnny Appleseed.

28
00:02:29.251 --> 00:02:36.335
People loved him because he was a kind and gentle man, and because he was generous with what little he had.

29
00:02:36.336 --> 00:02:43.144
However, as people continued to travel west, stories about Johnny Appleseed sprouted up everywhere.

30
00:02:43.145 --> 00:02:51.874
Some said that he used to talk to animals while he worked, and that he was such a gentle man the animals actually listened.

31
00:02:51.875 --> 00:02:58.202
In one story, a snowstorm took Johnny Appleseed by surprise, and he had no warm place to sleep.

32
00:02:58.203 --> 00:03:06.323
So he crawled into a hollow log with a mother bear and her cubs and spent the night, or so the story goes.

33
00:03:06.324 --> 00:03:12.060
Another story said that because Johnny Appleseed didn't wear any shoes, he had very tough feet.

34
00:03:12.061 --> 00:03:21.705
In fact, his feet were so tough that one afternoon, while he was working in the field, a rattlesnake tried to bite him but couldn't get through his skin!

35
00:03:21.706 --> 00:03:26.573
There is no historical record to prove that any of these Johnny Appleseed stories are true.

36
00:03:26.574 --> 00:03:30.107
Still, all accounts do agree about John Chapman's character.

37
00:03:30.108 --> 00:03:34.516
He was a man who was kind to the Indians and sincere in his Christian faith.

38
00:03:34.517 --> 00:03:42.795
He loved God, and would happily tell anyone about God as he sold them apple trees or gave them some apples to eat.

39
00:03:42.796 --> 00:03:52.327
John faithfully planted apple trees all across the wilderness of America, which later became the states of Indiana, Michigan, Ohio, and Illinois.

40
00:03:52.328 --> 00:03:57.193
No one knows how many trees he planted, but the count is definitely in the millions.

41
00:03:57.194 --> 00:04:05.762
Despite all the trees he planted, he was a very poor man who lived simply, but he seemed to like it that way.

42
00:04:05.763 --> 00:04:13.263
He was welcomed at any settler's table for dinner, and would happily play with the children and read them Bible stories when the meal was finished.

43
00:04:13.264 --> 00:04:17.661
Somewhere around the age of seventy, John Chapman died of the winter plague.

44
00:04:17.662 --> 00:04:22.106
Settlers mourned his death, but his generosity blooms each spring.

45
00:04:22.107 --> 00:04:28.070
As apple trees sprout up across the nation, the legend of Johnny Appleseed lives on.

